# --- added 2026-08-27 ---------------------------------------------------
# Build output and downloaded data, none of it source. rust/target alone is
# over a gigabyte and contains files GitHub would reject outright, and it was
# not covered by the /rust_processor/target/ line above.
rust/target/
.DS_Store
_build_paper/
_build_addfiles/
scripts/vina_1.2.6_mac_x86_64
scripts/vina_1.2.6_mac_x86_64.zip

# QM9 as downloaded by torch_geometric: 589 MB, regenerated by
# process_and_train.py on first run. gdb9.sdf and data_v3.pt are each far over
# GitHub's file limit.
data/QM9/

# A probe output kept in the archive by accident; over 20 MB and derived.
research_archive/e1d07839/probe.csv
.harness_unrestored

# --- generated output: never in git ------------------------------------------
# Everything under results/ is produced by a run. It was tracked, the cluster
# has its own copies of the same paths, and every `git pull` there aborted with
# "untracked working tree files would be overwritten" -- a wall of file names
# that has to be cleared by hand before anything can be pulled. Untracked
# 2026-08-28. Nothing is lost: the files stay on disk everywhere they already
# are, and every version remains in history --
#     git checkout <commit> -- results/<path>
# brings any of them back.
results/
data/smiles_db.sqlite

# Generated job scripts. The GENERATORS (generate_scripts.py and friends) and the
# runbooks stay in git -- they are the source. The .sh files they emit do not:
# they are rebuilt from the generator every time, the cluster has its own copies
# at the same paths, and every `git pull` there aborted on them. Untracked
# 2026-08-28, same reason as results/.
slurm_scripts_*/*.sh

# Written by `cargo build` on whichever machine built last, and the cluster's
# copy differs from the laptop's. Tracking it bought reproducibility nobody was
# using and cost a merge conflict on every pull.
rust/Cargo.lock

# Scheduler logs.
slurm-*.out
*_%j.out
